\date{2026-09-03}
\title{Flat Segal types are simplicial}
\author{ulrikbuchholtz}
\taxon{Module}
\import{stt-macros}

\transclude{stt-00AB}

\p{A crisp [Segal type](stt-00AI) is [simplicial](stt-00AC), granted that
[cubes separate](stt-00J7) and that [simplicial stability](ub-000Z) holds
(\ref{ub-003F}). The argument is from \citek{SGWB26}, based on initial ideas
by Gratzer and Schellingerhout. It runs through the
[ordered subcube](ub-000Y) #{\mathrm{Ord}_n \subseteq \square^n}, the part
of the cube on which the coordinates are pairwise comparable, and three facts
about it.}

\ol{
\li{Its inclusion into the cube is inner anodyne (\ref{ub-0030}), so every
Segal type is orthogonal to it. This is the combinatorial heart, proved in
[the module on convex subcubes](Synthetic.Categories.ConvexSubcubes) from a
retraction of the cube onto a convex subcube.}
\li{Every simplicial type is orthogonal to it too (\ref{ub-003E}), for the
different reason that it is null at each comparability.}
\li{A crisp map that is an equivalence on [figures](ub-0032) of every simplex
is one on figures of every ordered subcube
(\ref{ub-003D}), because the ordered subcube is
[glued from simplices](Synthetic.Categories.OrderedGlueing). Given that cubes
separate, such a map is an equivalence once its source and target are
orthogonal to the ordered subcube (\ref{ub-0010}).}
}

\p{Apply the third to the unit #{\eta : A \to \bigcirc A} of the simplicial
reflection: stability says that #{\eta} is an equivalence on simplex-figures,
and the first two
facts apply to #{A} and #{\bigcirc A}. So #{\eta} is an equivalence.}

\agda{
