\subtree[ub-000Y]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The ordered subcube}

\p{The \em{ordered} part of the cube #{\square^n} is where its coordinates
are pairwise comparable,}

##{\mathrm{Ordered}(v) = \forall i, j < n.\ v_i \le v_j \lor v_j \le v_i,
   \qquad \mathrm{Ord}_n = \textstyle\sum_{v : \square^n} \mathrm{Ordered}(v).}

\p{Formally it is [\code{Everywhere}](ub-002Z), the comparability of every
pair. In the model it is the union of the #{n!} simplices of the cube, its
triangulation; internally, a finite set of pairwise comparable elements is a
chain, by a sorting induction that may destruct each #{\lor} because the goal
is a proposition.}

\agda{
Ordered : ∀ {n} → □^ n → Type 𝓘
Ordered = Everywhere

Ordered-is-prop : ∀ {n} (v : □^ n) → is-prop (Ordered v)
Ordered-is-prop = Everywhere-is-prop

ordered-incl : (n : ℕ) → Σ (□^ n) (Ordered {n}) → □^ n
ordered-incl n = fst
}

\p{In dimension two #{\mathrm{Ord}_2} is the union of the two triangles, that
is, the [triangulated square](stt-00CC) (\ref{ub-000F}): there is one pair of
distinct coordinates to compare, and a coordinate is comparable with itself by
reflexivity.}

\agda{
private
  ord₂→ : (v : □^ 2) → Ordered {2} v → generating-fam-is-simplicial v
  ord₂→ v h = h (fsuc fzero) fzero

  ord₂← : (v : □^ 2) → generating-fam-is-simplicial v → Ordered {2} v
  ord₂← v p fzero fzero = ι₁ ≤-refl
  ord₂← v p fzero (fsuc fzero)
    = *-prop-rec (generating-fam-is-simplicial-is-prop _) ι₂ ι₁ p
  ord₂← v p (fsuc fzero) fzero = p
  ord₂← v p (fsuc fzero) (fsuc fzero) = ι₁ ≤-refl

ordered₂≃ : Σ (□^ 2) (Ordered {2}) ≃ Σ (□^ 2) generating-fam-is-simplicial
ordered₂≃
  = sub-incl≃ Ordered-is-prop generating-fam-is-simplicial-is-prop ord₂→ ord₂←
}
}

\subtree[ub-0030]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The ordered subcube in every dimension}

\p{The inclusion #{\mathrm{Ord}_n \hookrightarrow \square^n} is inner anodyne
for every #{n}. The proof is
[the module on convex subcubes](Synthetic.Categories.ConvexSubcubes):}

\ol{
\li{[the clamp](ub-002F) retracts the cube onto a convex subtype while fixing
two chosen coordinates, so adjoining
[the comparability of a pair over a convex base](ub-002M) is inner anodyne;}
\li{a recursion on a list of pairs adjoins them all, splitting each
comparability into its two orientations and glueing (\ref{ub-002Y});}
\li{and enumerating every pair turns the list into the quantifier
(\ref{ub-002Z}).}
}

\p{All that is left here is the empty cube, where being ordered is a condition
on no pairs at all.}

\agda{
ordered-incl-is-inner-anodyne
  : (n : ℕ) → is-inner-anodyne (ordered-incl n)
ordered-incl-is-inner-anodyne zero
  = is-anodyne←is-equiv Λ[2,1]-incl
      (singleton-fst-is-equiv λ v → mk-singl (λ ()) (Ordered-is-prop v (λ ())))
ordered-incl-is-inner-anodyne (suc m) = Everywhere-is-inner-anodyne
}

\p{So a Segal type is orthogonal to it, and a crisp one is #{\flat}-orthogonal
to it, which is what \ref{ub-0010} asks of the source and target.}

\agda{
ordered-incl-precomp-is-equiv
  : ∀ {𝓤} {A : Type 𝓤} → is-segal A → (n : ℕ)
  → is-equiv (precomp A (ordered-incl n))
ordered-incl-precomp-is-equiv {A = A} seg n
  = is-orthogonal↔orthogonal-! .fst
      (ordered-incl-is-inner-anodyne n (!: A)
        (is-orthogonal↔orthogonal-! .snd (seg tt)))

♭-ordered-incl-precomp-is-equiv
  : ∀ {@♭ 𝓤} {@♭ A : Type 𝓤} → @♭ is-segal A
  → ∀ {@♭ n} → is-equiv (♭-map (precomp A (ordered-incl n)))
♭-ordered-incl-precomp-is-equiv seg {n}
  = is-♭-equiv←is-equiv (ordered-incl-precomp-is-equiv seg n)
}
}

\subtree[ub-0010]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Cubes separate through the ordered subcube}

\p{Suppose #{X} and #{Y} are #{\flat}-orthogonal to
#{\iota : \mathrm{Ord}_n \hookrightarrow \square^n} in every dimension: that
is, restriction along #{\iota} is an equivalence after #{\modflat{-}}. Given
that [cubes separate](stt-00J7), a crisp #{f : X \to Y} is an equivalence as
soon as it is one on crisp points and on [#{\mathrm{Ord}_n}-figures](ub-0032)
for every #{n}. This lemma does not use
[simplicial stability](ub-000Z).}

\p{The proof is the square}

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
	{\modflat{(\square^n \to X)}} && {\modflat{(\square^n \to Y)}} \\
	\\
	{\modflat{(\mathrm{Ord}_n \to X)}} && {\modflat{(\mathrm{Ord}_n \to Y)}}
	\arrow["{f \circ -}", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["{- \circ \iota}"', from=1-1, to=3-1]
	\arrow["{- \circ \iota}", from=1-3, to=3-3]
	\arrow["{f \circ -}"', from=3-1, to=3-3]
\end{tikzcd}
}

\p{which commutes on the nose — both composites send #{g} to
#{f \circ g \circ \iota} — so that #{\modflat{-}} of it commutes by
#{\flat}-induction. The two verticals are equivalences by orthogonality and the
bottom by hypothesis, so the top is one by three-for-two, and then
[cubes separate](stt-00UV) gives that #{f} is an equivalence.}

\p{The condition on the ordered subcube can be traded for one on simplices,
since a crisp map that is an equivalence on simplex-figures is one on
ordered-subcube-figures
(\ref{ub-003D}). That is a colimit argument rather than a lifting one, and it
is the content of [the module on glueing simplices](Synthetic.Categories.OrderedGlueing).}

\agda{
module _
  {@♭ 𝓤 𝓥} {@♭ X : Type 𝓤} {@♭ Y : Type 𝓥} (@♭ f : X → Y)
  where

  private
    ordered-square
      : ∀ {@♭ n}
      → ♭-map (precomp Y (ordered-incl n)) ∘ ♭-map (postcomp (□^ n) f)
      ~ ♭-map (postcomp (Σ (□^ n) (Ordered {n})) f)
        ∘ ♭-map (precomp X (ordered-incl n))
    ordered-square (mod♭ g) = refl

  is-equiv←ordered-cubes
    : @♭ family-separates (□^_)
    → @♭ (∀ {@♭ n} → is-equiv (♭-map (precomp X (ordered-incl n))))
    → @♭ (∀ {@♭ n} → is-equiv (♭-map (precomp Y (ordered-incl n))))
    → @♭ is-equiv (♭-map f)
    → @♭ (∀ {@♭ n} → is-♭-equiv-on (Σ (□^ n) (Ordered {n})) f)
    → is-equiv f
  is-equiv←ordered-cubes sep oX oY f♭ fOrd
    = sep (mk-arrow f) f♭
        (λ {n} → 3-for-2 oY (is-equiv←~ ordered-square (is-equiv-∘ fOrd oX)))

  is-equiv←ordered-simplices
    : @♭ family-separates (□^_)
    → @♭ (∀ {@♭ n} → is-equiv (♭-map (precomp X (ordered-incl n))))
    → @♭ (∀ {@♭ n} → is-equiv (♭-map (precomp Y (ordered-incl n))))
    → @♭ is-equiv (♭-map f)
    → @♭ (∀ {@♭ n} → is-♭-equiv-on (Δ^ n) f)
    → is-equiv f
  is-equiv←ordered-simplices sep oX oY f♭ fΔ
    = is-equiv←ordered-cubes sep oX oY f♭
        (λ {n} → is-♭-equiv-on-ordered-subcube f (λ k → fΔ {k}) n)
}
}

\subtree[ub-000Z]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{The simplicial stability axiom}

\p{Simplicial types are the [nullification](stt-00AC) at the family
#{(i \le j) \lor (j \le i)}, so they form a reflective subuniverse with unit
#{\eta : A \to \bigcirc A}. \em{Simplicial stability} is the statement that the
reflection changes nothing on crisp simplices: for every crisp #{A}, the unit
#{\eta} is an equivalence on [#{\Delta^n}-figures](ub-0032) for every crisp
#{n},}

##{\modflat{(\eta \circ -)} :
   \modflat{(\Delta^n \to A)} \longrightarrow \modflat{(\Delta^n \to \bigcirc A)}}

\p{is an equivalence. Like the fact that [cubes separate](stt-00UV), this is a
property of the intended model rather than a consequence of the axioms
available here, so it is a definition, to be taken as a hypothesis, and not a
postulate. \ref{ub-0043} explains what it says in the model.}

\p{A type is simplicial exactly when its unit is an equivalence, which is
how stability will be used.}

\agda{
is-simplicial←η-is-equiv
  : ∀ {𝓤} {A : Type 𝓤} → is-equiv (Simp.η {A = A}) → is-simplicial A
is-simplicial←η-is-equiv {A = A} = in-SubU←η-is-equiv (Simp.reflector A)
}

\agda{
simplicial-stability : Typeω
simplicial-stability
  = ∀ {@♭ 𝓤} {@♭ A : Type 𝓤} {@♭ n : ℕ}
  → is-♭-equiv-on (Δ^ n) (Simp.η {A = A})
}
}

\subtree[ub-0043]{
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Stability is a finite shadow of cubical generation}
\date{2026-09-06}

\p{Here is what [simplicial stability](ub-000Z) says in the model, and why it
is the right shape for an axiom. Three toposes sit inside one another, all
presheaves on full subcategories of finite posets with monotone maps: the
classifying topos of distributive lattices #{\mathrm{Set}[\mathrm{DLat}]} is
presheaves on finite posets, since finitely presented distributive lattices
are the downset lattices of finite posets; cubical spaces are presheaves on
finite lattices, the idempotent completion of the cube category, and
classify the flat lattices; and simplicial spaces are presheaves
on finite chains. Each inclusion of sites induces a geometric embedding whose
inverse image is restriction and whose direct image is right Kan extension,
and the interval is the representable #{y([1])} in all three.}

\p{Simplicial types are the sheaves for the coverage of a cube by the loci
#{\{f \le g\}}, #{\{g \le f\}}, and that subtopos is the innermost of the
three: so on a crisp type #{A}, the reflection #{\bigcirc A} is the right Kan
extension of the restriction of #{A} to chains,
#{(\bigcirc A)(L) = \lim_{[k] \to L} A([k])}. The simplex #{\Delta^n} is the
representable #{y([n])}, a chain. So}

##{\modflat{(\Delta^n \to \bigcirc A)} = (\bigcirc A)([n]) = A([n]) =
   \modflat{(\Delta^n \to A)},}

\p{which is stability, and in the model it is the statement that restricting
a right Kan extension back to the subcategory returns what was extended: a
tautology, once #{\bigcirc} is known to be that extension.}

\p{What is not a tautology is the identification of #{\bigcirc}. It rests on
\em{cubical generation}: every cubical space is the colimit of its cubes, and
mapping out of a chain commutes with that colimit because a chain is a
retract of a cube. That is the co-Yoneda lemma for the site, and its
internalisation would be a colimit indexed by the category of crisp cubes of
#{A}, with the coherence data that an #{\infty}-categorical diagram requires
— which the type theory has no way to write down. Stability is the shadow
of generation on the representable chains: finitely many instances per
dimension, each a single equivalence, and no coherence at all. The
[cubes separate](stt-00J7) axiom is the other shadow: the restriction
#{A \mapsto (n \mapsto \modflat{(\square^n \to A)})} is conservative. The two
are independent in one direction at least: #{\mathrm{Set}[\mathrm{DLat}]}
satisfies stability, since #{\bigcirc} is right Kan extension from chains
there too, and not separation, since a presheaf on finite posets is not
determined on lattices.}

\p{Two consequences of the picture. Stability at the cubes fails: the
reflection of the split square #{S} is the square, so
#{\modflat{(\square^2 \to S)} \to \modflat{(\square^2 \to \bigcirc S)}}
misses the identity; the internal retraction #{\square^n \to \Delta^n} by
sorting shows that stability at simplices would follow from stability at
cubes, and this is why it cannot. And the general form of stability, that for
every crisp finite lattice #{L} the maps #{N(L) \to \bigcirc A} are computed
by the limit over the simplices of #{N(L)} of the maps #{\Delta^k \to A},
follows from the stated form together with \ref{ub-003E} and
\ref{ub-003D}, so stability at chains is the minimal shadow to take.}
}

\subtree[ub-003E]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Simplicial types are orthogonal to the ordered subcube}

\p{Every simplicial type is orthogonal to
#{\mathrm{Ord}_n \hookrightarrow \square^n}. This is the counterpart of
\ref{ub-0030} for Segal types, but it holds for a different reason: nullity
rather than lifting. Being ordered is a \em{finite conjunction} of
comparabilities, and a type null at each of two propositions is null at their
conjunction (\code{is-null-at-×}, from
[the closure of null types under sums](stt-00L9)). So a simplicial type is
null at #{\mathrm{Ordered}(v)} for every #{v}, and orthogonality to the
inclusion is exactly that, fibrewise, once the map is curried.}

\p{The conjunction is finite because it is indexed by pairs of coordinates.
Reading it off the quantifier would need a product over #{\Fin n} broken into
factors; reading it off [the list of all pairs](ub-002X) instead makes it an
iterated #{\times} on the nose, and \ref{ub-002Z} says the two agree.}

\agda{
module _ {𝓤} {S : Type 𝓤} (simp : is-simplicial S) where

  private
    null-Cmp : ∀ {n} (K : Rels n) → is-null (λ (v : □^ n) → Cmp K v) S
    null-Cmp [] v = sing-const-is-equiv 𝟙ᴸ-is-singleton
    null-Cmp ((i , j) ∷ K)
      = is-null-at-× (λ v → simp (□-coord v i , □-coord v j)) (null-Cmp K)

    null-Ordered : ∀ {n} → is-null (λ (v : □^ n) → Ordered {n} v) S
    null-Ordered {n}
      = is-null-at-equiv
          (λ v → mk≃ (Everywhere→Cmp v)
                   (is-equiv←qinv
                     ( Cmp→Everywhere v
                     , (λ _ → Everywhere-is-prop v _ _)
                     , (λ _ → Cmp-is-prop (all-pairs n) v _ _))))
          (null-Cmp (all-pairs n))

  ordered-incl-precomp-is-equiv←is-simplicial
    : (n : ℕ) → is-equiv (precomp S (ordered-incl n))
  ordered-incl-precomp-is-equiv←is-simplicial n
    = 3-for-2 (curry≃ ._≃_.fwd-is-equiv)
        (is-equiv←~ ~refl (postcomp-Π-equiv (λ {v} → null-Ordered v)))
}
}

\subtree[ub-003F]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Flat Segal types are simplicial}

\p{A crisp [Segal type](stt-00AI) is [simplicial](stt-00AC), granted that
[cubes separate](stt-00J7) and that [simplicial stability](ub-000Z) holds.}

\p{This is the only place stability is used. The argument compares #{A}
with its reflection #{\bigcirc A} along the unit #{\eta}, applying
\ref{ub-0010} to #{\eta}. That needs four things:}

\ol{
\li{#{A} is #{\flat}-orthogonal to #{\mathrm{Ord}_n \hookrightarrow \square^n},
by \ref{ub-0030}, since #{A} is Segal;}
\li{so is #{\bigcirc A}, by \ref{ub-003E}, since #{\bigcirc A} is simplicial;}
\li{#{\modflat{\eta}} is an equivalence, which is stability at #{n = 0}, as
#{\Delta^0} is contractible;}
\li{#{\eta} is an equivalence on simplex-figures, which is stability itself.}
}

\p{The first two hold for entirely different reasons, one by lifting and one
by nullity. So #{\eta} is an equivalence, and a type whose unit is an
equivalence is simplicial (\ref{ub-000Z}).}

\agda{
module _ {@♭ 𝓤} {@♭ A : Type 𝓤} where

  private
    ○-ordered
      : ∀ {@♭ n} → is-equiv (♭-map (precomp (Simp.○ A) (ordered-incl n)))
    ○-ordered {n}
      = is-♭-equiv←is-equiv
          (ordered-incl-precomp-is-equiv←is-simplicial Simp.○∈S n)

    const-Δ^0 : ∀ {𝓥} {B : Type 𝓥} → is-equiv (const: B (Δ^ 0))
    const-Δ^0 = sing-const-is-equiv Δ⁰-is-singleton

    η-square
      : ♭-map (const: (Simp.○ A) (Δ^ 0)) ∘ ♭-map (Simp.η {A = A})
      ~ ♭-map (postcomp (Δ^ 0) (Simp.η {A = A})) ∘ ♭-map (const: A (Δ^ 0))
    η-square (mod♭ a) = refl

    η-♭ : @♭ simplicial-stability → is-equiv (♭-map (Simp.η {A = A}))
    η-♭ stab
      = 3-for-2 (is-♭-equiv←is-equiv const-Δ^0)
          (is-equiv←~ η-square
            (is-equiv-∘ (stab {A = A} {n = 0}) (is-♭-equiv←is-equiv const-Δ^0)))

  is-simplicial←♭-ordered-orthogonal
    : @♭ family-separates (□^_)
    → @♭ simplicial-stability
    → @♭ (∀ {@♭ n} → is-equiv (♭-map (precomp A (ordered-incl n))))
    → is-simplicial A
  is-simplicial←♭-ordered-orthogonal sep stab oA
    = is-simplicial←η-is-equiv
        (is-equiv←ordered-simplices (Simp.η {A = A}) sep oA ○-ordered
          (η-♭ stab) (λ {n} → stab {A = A} {n = n}))

  is-simplicial←is-segal-ordered
    : @♭ family-separates (□^_)
    → @♭ simplicial-stability
    → @♭ is-segal A
    → is-simplicial A
  is-simplicial←is-segal-ordered sep stab seg
    = is-simplicial←♭-ordered-orthogonal sep stab
        (♭-ordered-incl-precomp-is-equiv seg)
}
}
